{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Sarah conducts an experiment $40$ times to see how quickly a machine sorts a bin of parts. The table below shows the results of her experiments.\n\n| Time (seconds) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| $<20$ | $6$ |\n| $20-30$ | $16$ |\n| $30-40$ | $10$ |\n| $40-50$ | $5$ |\n| $>50$ | $3$ |\n\nBased on the experimental probabilities of these results, complete the following statements.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-3}}\na machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds.",
  "worked_solution": "One event is more likely than another if it has a greater probability of occurring. If two events have the same probability, they are equally likely.\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a machine sorted a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds $(6+16=22)$ by the total number of times Sarah conducted the experiment $(40).$ This gives\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }<30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{22}{40}.\n$$\n\nThis probability is a relatively large number, closer to $1.$ Since\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}<\\frac{22}{40}<1,\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\text{likely}.$\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a machine sorted a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds $(10+5+3=18)$ by the total number of times Sarah conducted the experiment $(40).$ This gives\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }>30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{18}{40}.\n$$\n\nThis probability is a small number, closer to $0.$ Since\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{18}{40}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\text{unlikely}.$\n\n- The experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is *greater than* the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }<30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{22}{40}>\\frac{18}{40}=P(\\text{sorts in }>30\\text{ seconds})\n$$\n\nTherefore, a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n$\\text{more likely than}$\na machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds.",
